WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.462 --> 00:00:03.379
[music]

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.320
[Instructor] Cool, so we have
our player set up

3
00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:11.320
as much as possible
for now,

4
00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:15.403
so in the past we've commonly
gotten just the gameObject

5
00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:18.160
or the Transform Component,
whatever we wanted

6
00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.170
to call any methods.

7
00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:23.030
But if we want to actually
control the movement

8
00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:25.545
and the Gravity of the player,
and all of that,

9
00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:29.380
we actually need to use
a new way to get a Component

10
00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:32.210
for our "Rigidbody,"
because Rigidbodies aren't

11
00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:35.690
something that are automatically
on other gameObjects

12
00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:38.856
versus something like a Transform,
which is always on gameObjects.

13
00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.745
So we'll do that using
our PlayerController Script.

14
00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:44.200
So in our PlayerController Script,

15
00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.080
we're going to create
a new variable.

16
00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.240
I already know that I don't need
this to be seen

17
00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:50.575
by anything outside of this class,

18
00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:52.920
so I'm going to name
this private for now.

19
00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:56.562
And then, it's actually
going to be our Rigidbody.

20
00:00:58.515 --> 00:01:01.203
So this will be the Rigidbody
Component on our Player

21
00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:04.880
similar to how we had
attached gameObjects before

22
00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:06.890
but in this case,
this is a Rigidbody.

23
00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:10.990
And we're going to just
name this "playerRb,"

24
00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:13.000
for Player Rigidbody.

25
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.162
Now versus something
like transform

26
00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:18.585
where we can already get access
to our transform Component,

27
00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:22.110
we don't automatically get access
to our Rigidbody Component

28
00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:24.636
because that's not something
on every single gameObject.

29
00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:29.192
So in order to do that,
we actually need to use

30
00:01:29.802 --> 00:01:31.940
something called
"GetComponent<>()".

31
00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:37.491
So if we take our playerRb
variable for our Rigidbody,

32
00:01:39.081 --> 00:01:45.559
and now if we assign that or set
that to the Component of our Rigidbody,

33
00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:48.719
so we use a method
called GetComponent<>().

34
00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:56.458
Now we have to use these left
and right carets here,

35
00:01:57.504 --> 00:02:01.310
which is different than using
parentheses to pass in parameters.

36
00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:03.870
Specifically, the right and left carets

37
00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:06.322
actually get you
a type of something,

38
00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:10.185
so for example,
in this case we're getting a Rigidbody,

39
00:02:11.545 --> 00:02:15.770
but you could use these carets
to find the type of anything,

40
00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:18.350
so if we actually want
to get a Collider, for example,

41
00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:21.090
that was on our Player,
we could do that too.

42
00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:25.120
So the carets are specifically
for finding different things like...

43
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:28.766
our components that are
on our different objects.

44
00:02:30.216 --> 00:02:32.520
Now we do need to close
this with a parentheses

45
00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:35.773
because it is actually a call
that we're making as a method.

46
00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:39.683
And we'll close that statement.
And then, just to test and see

47
00:02:39.683 --> 00:02:42.245
if this works if we actually
got that Rigidbody,

48
00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:46.577
we're going to apply
a force to our Rigidbody.

49
00:02:52.127 --> 00:02:54.570
Now because we're using
a Rigidbody Component,

50
00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:57.311
it already has a method
called "AddForce()."...

51
00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:06.670
And you can think of this
similar as "Transform.Translate(),"

52
00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:09.830
but instead of moving
something physically

53
00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:13.080
just using positions,
we can apply forces to things

54
00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:16.370
just like we would in the real world
using physics to make something move.

55
00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:19.130
So in this case, we're going
to make our Player move up

56
00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:24.237
just to see what happens,
so we're going to use "Vector3.up,"...

57
00:03:26.157 --> 00:03:28.150
and we're going to amplify
this by a lot,

58
00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:30.100
so we'll just multiply
this by 100.

59
00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:37.812
And then we'll save,
and now in Unity

60
00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:42.174
if you will look at our Game view,
when I press Play

61
00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:48.477
the character jumped very slightly.

62
00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:53.091
Let's see if we can make
that a little bit more powerful.

63
00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:56.422
Let's set this to something like 1000.

64
00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:02.515
And we'll save, and now back
in Unity we'll press Play.

65
00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:07.810
There you go.
That's a lot of force.

66
00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:10.250
And then because he has Gravity

67
00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:12.550
the character falls all the
way back down to the ground.

68
00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:14.580
We'll do that one more time.

69
00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:18.730
Whoo! It's like he's so excited.

70
00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:22.415
Cool.

71
00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:27.220
So what you're going to do is going
into your PlayerController Script.

72
00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:29.800
In your PlayerController
Script you're going to make

73
00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:33.820
a private Rigidbody
variable called playerRb

74
00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:38.078
for the player Rigidbody,
and then in your Start() method

75
00:04:38.518 --> 00:04:40.775
you're going to assign that Rigidbody

76
00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:45.570
to our Rigidbody Component using

77
00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:48.560
the GetComponent<>() method,
so that it can find

78
00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:51.960
the Rigidbody on the player,
and then we're going

79
00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:54.450
to apply a force
to that Player so that we can

80
00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:58.140
see that they're actually
jumping in the air using forces.

81
00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:02.669
Now it's your turn. [music]
